-- The store gets a clock.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS
--
-- Written when this database had one scheduled job in it, `compliance-expiry-reminders`.
-- Everything "automatic" about the store is really advanced by somebody opening
-- a page — fulfilment retries when an administrator loads the Orders screen,
-- tracking by a button, the supplier catalogue when somebody remembers. So
-- overnight and at weekends the store does nothing, and a paid order that fails
-- to reach its supplier sits unshipped with the failure written to a field on a
-- record nobody opens. That happened, and nobody heard it.
--
-- ONE JOB, NOT FIVE
--
-- `/store/cron-tick` runs a registry of small jobs, each individually
-- switchable from `/store/autonomy/settings`. One schedule, one secret, one
-- thing to rotate. A job can be switched off without touching this file, and
-- this file can be unscheduled without editing any job.
--
-- ALREADY RUNNING IN PRODUCTION. THIS FILE IS THE RECORD, NOT THE SWITCH.
--
-- Both jobs below have been live since 2026-10-03 — they were applied by hand,
-- the way the rest of this history was written, and this file was never updated
-- to match. Checked against `cron.job` on 2026-10-15: `store-cron-tick` every
-- fifteen minutes and `store-watchdog` at seven past the hour, both succeeding.
--
-- It had drifted three ways, and arming it as it stood would have done real
-- damage rather than nothing:
--
--   job name    the file said `store-tick`, production runs `store-cron-tick`,
--               so `cron.unschedule` would have matched nothing and the
--               `cron.schedule` below would have added a SECOND tick against
--               /store/cron-tick alongside the live one
--   schedule    the watchdog said `*/30 * * * *`, production runs `7 * * * *`
--   timeout     neither job set `timeout_milliseconds`, and both live ones are
--               at 30000
--
-- That last one is the dangerous one. `20261013150000_cron_jobs_explicit_timeout`
-- exists because pg_net's default is five seconds and an on-call tick was
-- missed to it; that migration skipped the store jobs precisely because they
-- already had the timeout. Re-running this file would have taken it back off
-- them. All three are now corrected to match what is live.
--
-- It keeps the `.pending` suffix, but the reason has changed: not "do not arm
-- this yet" but "there is nothing left to arm". Renaming it is now harmless
-- rather than harmful — unschedule-then-schedule with identical definitions —
-- and that is the point of making it match. Do not reach for `supabase db push`
-- to do it; see the note in `20261015120000_exchange_listing_logo.sql` for why
-- that command is unsafe in this repository.
--
-- WHAT IT WILL DO ON THE FIRST DAY: NOTHING
--
-- Read this before deciding it is risky. The default settings enable the
-- heartbeat and nothing else. The heartbeat looks at nothing and changes
-- nothing; it writes a record saying the clock is alive and lists which jobs
-- are registered and switched off. Fulfilment, tracking, catalogue sync and
-- cart recovery all start disabled and are switched on one at a time, after the
-- clock has been watched working.
--
-- That is the point of arming it empty. If the schedule, the secret, the header
-- or the mount path is wrong, it is wrong while doing nothing — and
-- `/store/autonomy/status` is how you tell "working and idle" apart from "never
-- ran". There is no way to learn that from a clock that was armed carrying the
-- fulfilment sweep.
--
-- THE TRAP THIS ONE AVOIDS
--
-- `/store/cron-tick` and `/autopilot/cron-tick` are both listed in
-- `PUBLIC_POST_PATHS` in index.tsx. They have to be: the auth gate there
-- defaults every unlisted route to "signed in", a scheduler has no user to be,
-- and the gate answers **401 "Sign in required."** before a route's own secret
-- check ever runs. `/autopilot/cron-tick` was NOT listed, so arming its
-- migration would have failed on every single tick with nothing to show for it.
-- Both are listed now. Anything else scheduled against this server needs the
-- same entry — this is the third time the trap has come up.

-- The secret the scheduler sends and the route checks. Generated here so it
-- never has to be typed anywhere, and kept in the same private table the
-- compliance secret already uses: no grants to anon or authenticated, and row
-- level security enabled on 2026-10-03.
insert into private_cron_config (key, value)
values (
  'store_cron_secret',
  encode(gen_random_bytes(32), 'base64')
)
on conflict (key) do nothing;

-- Unschedule first so re-running this is safe.
select cron.unschedule('store-cron-tick')
where exists (select 1 from cron.job where jobname = 'store-cron-tick');

-- Every fifteen minutes.
--
-- The tick takes a lease before it runs and skips if a previous one is still
-- holding it, so a slow supplier API cannot have two ticks overlap. Every job
-- is idempotent in its own right as well, because a lease in a store with no
-- compare-and-set is a second line of defence rather than a guarantee.
--
-- Fifteen minutes is close enough that a customer is told about their parcel
-- within the hour, without asking the function to wake up constantly.
select cron.schedule(
  'store-cron-tick',
  '*/15 * * * *',
  $$
  select net.http_post(
    url     := 'https://plzsvzwwcdopnawtiwzm.supabase.co/functions/v1/make-server-3eae23a6/store/cron-tick',
    headers := jsonb_build_object(
      'Content-Type', 'application/json',
      'Authorization', 'Bearer ' || (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'anon_key'),
      'X-Store-Cron-Secret', (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'store_cron_secret')
    ),
    body    := '{}'::jsonb,
    timeout_milliseconds := 30000
  );
  $$
);

-- THE DEAD-MAN'S SWITCH, AND WHY IT IS A SECOND JOB
--
-- Everything above argues for one schedule rather than five, and this is the
-- one exception. A watchdog inside the thing it watches is not a watchdog: if
-- `store-cron-tick` stops firing, nothing inside it runs either, including any check
-- on whether it is running. So the staleness check has to be triggered by
-- something else.
--
-- It is cheap and it cannot cause harm: it reads one record, compares a
-- timestamp, and sends at most one email a day per state. It places no orders
-- and changes no prices. Hourly against a one-hour silence threshold means
-- a stopped clock is noticed within about two hours.
--
-- WHAT NEITHER OF THESE CAN CATCH
--
-- If pg_cron itself stops, or the project is paused, nothing fires — including
-- the watchdog. No in-database watchdog can report its own absence. The only
-- real protection is something outside this system checking in: an external
-- uptime monitor pointed at GET /store/autonomy/status would close that last
-- gap. Until there is one, "no alarm" is not quite the same as "all well", and
-- that is worth knowing rather than assuming.

select cron.unschedule('store-watchdog')
where exists (select 1 from cron.job where jobname = 'store-watchdog');

select cron.schedule(
  'store-watchdog',
  '7 * * * *',
  $$
  select net.http_post(
    url     := 'https://plzsvzwwcdopnawtiwzm.supabase.co/functions/v1/make-server-3eae23a6/store/watchdog',
    headers := jsonb_build_object(
      'Content-Type', 'application/json',
      'Authorization', 'Bearer ' || (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'anon_key'),
      'X-Store-Cron-Secret', (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'store_cron_secret')
    ),
    body    := '{}'::jsonb,
    timeout_milliseconds := 30000
  );
  $$
);

-- AFTER ARMING THIS
--
-- 1. Wait fifteen minutes, then GET /store/autonomy/status as a member of
--    staff. `totalRuns` should be 1 or more and `minutesSinceLastRun` under 20.
--    If `secretConfigured` is false, the row above did not land.
-- 2. Leave it a full day. 96 heartbeats, no other effect.
-- 3. Then switch on `fulfil`, and only that, through
--    PUT /store/autonomy/settings. Watch one real order through.
--
-- TO STOP IT
--
--     select cron.unschedule('store-cron-tick');
--     select cron.unschedule('store-watchdog');
--
-- Stop the watchdog too, or it will correctly report that the clock has
-- stopped — every day, because it has.
--
-- Or switch every job off and leave the heartbeat running, which keeps the
-- record of the clock being alive while it does nothing else.
